    "report": "# Research-Gap Report: Federated Learning\n\n## Topic Landscape\n\nThe federated learning literature organises into roughly fourteen identifiable clusters spanning the period 2020\u20132023. The largest and most mature threads concern general FL challenges and survey work (30 papers, mean year 2021.3), healthcare/medical applications (27, 2022.3), and wireless/communication optimisation (26, 2020.2), signalling that system-level and application-layer concerns dominate the field. Privacy-preserving mechanisms (25, 2021.4), edge/IoT deployment (22, 2020.7), and personalisation (21, 2021.1) form a dense middle tier, while more specialised clusters\u2014blockchain-based FL (18, 2021.5), adversarial attacks and poisoning (15, 2020.8), IoT intrusion detection (15, 2022.6), non-IID data (13, 2021.0), and gradient-level attacks (10, 2020.8)\u2014represent narrower but active sub-fields. The two most recent cluster centroids (mean year 2023.0) sit in trust/privacy attacks and agricultural disease detection, hinting at emerging application frontiers.\n\n---\n\n## Validated Gaps\n\n### Gap 1 \u2014 Benchmarking FL Under Real-World Healthcare Data Heterogeneity\n\n**Why it is a gap.** The bridge between the \"data/research/challenges\" cluster (n = 30) and the \"healthcare/data/medical\" cluster (n = 27) is supported by 269 bridge papers and carries a prominence score of 0.14, yet verification queries (\"machine learning challenges healthcare data\"; \"federated learning medical models\") returned only 23 recent papers and a confidence of just 0.08. The verdict is **WEAK**: despite substantial background literature, rigorous, reproducible benchmarks that jointly address FL-specific challenges (non-IID distributions, client dropout, heterogeneous modalities) *within* realistic healthcare dataset conditions remain sparse relative to the bridge volume. The gap is real but early-stage.\n\n**Research Agenda**\n1. *Benchmark construction* \u2014 Curate or synthesise multi-site medical datasets (imaging, EHR, genomics) with documented heterogeneity profiles; establish standardised FL simulation protocols that vary client count, data imbalance, and missingness.\n2. *Challenge mapping* \u2014 Systematically evaluate how known FL failure modes (convergence under non-IID, communication overhead, stragglers) manifest differently across healthcare data modalities, producing a taxonomy of domain-specific failure patterns.\n3. *Baseline publication* \u2014 Release open benchmark results and evaluation tooling to enable reproducible comparison across future FL-in-healthcare proposals.\n\n---\n\n### Gap 2 \u2014 Principled Integration of Blockchain Accountability into FL Privacy Frameworks\n\n**Why it is a gap.** The bridge between \"data/research/challenges\" (n = 30) and \"blockchain/data/privacy\" (n = 18) rests on 260 bridge papers with a prominence of 0.12. Crucially, verification queries (\"federated learning blockchain privacy\"; \"machine learning blockchain data sharing\") surfaced only **9 recent papers**\u2014the lowest retrieval count among all validated gaps\u2014while returning a confidence of 0.64. The WEAK verdict combined with high confidence and minimal recent output indicates that, although conceptual proposals exist, the intersection of FL's formal privacy guarantees (e.g., differential privacy, secure aggregation) with blockchain's auditability and incentive mechanisms has not been rigorously formalised or experimentally validated at scale.\n\n**Research Agenda**\n1. *Threat-model alignment* \u2014 Formally characterise where blockchain's transparency properties conflict with or complement FL's privacy objectives (e.g., on-chain model updates vs. gradient privacy), producing a unified adversarial model.\n2. *Protocol design and proof* \u2014 Develop and formally verify lightweight blockchain-assisted FL protocols that provide verifiable aggregation without leaking participant-level information, targeting resource-constrained or cross-silo settings.\n3. *Empirical validation* \u2014 Implement prototype systems and benchmark communication overhead, latency, and privacy-utility trade-offs against blockchain-free FL baselines across at least two application domains (e.g., healthcare, IoT).\n\n---\n\n## Rejected Candidates\n\nSix candidate gaps were rejected due to substantial existing coverage: the combinations of FL challenges with wireless communication (2,618 recent papers), privacy-preserving FL (8,324), healthcare with wireless (3,046), healthcare with privacy (116, borderline but above threshold), FL challenges with edge/IoT (3,623), and FL challenges with personalisation (809) all demonstrated sufficient recent literature to preclude genuine gap status."
